const protagorasSections: ArticleSection[] = [
  section('recovery', 'A famous sentence inside a lost body of work', [
    'Protagoras of Abdera is securely recognizable as a prominent fifth-century BCE teacher, but almost none of his writings survives in continuous form. Short formulations reach us because later authors quote or paraphrase them; larger positions are reconstructed chiefly from Plato, Aristotle, Sextus Empiricus, and Diogenes Laertius. Those witnesses are philosophically interested and separated from Protagoras by different distances. A responsible account therefore does not begin by calling him “the relativist.” It begins by asking which words are plausibly his, which arguments a critic builds around them, and which biographical stories first appear in late tradition.',
    'The losses are especially consequential because ancient titles are unstable. The work containing the measure sentence is called Truth in some evidence and Overthrowing Arguments in other evidence, perhaps as an alternative title; On the Gods is known principally through its opening statement; lists in Diogenes name works that no longer survive. A title can show that a topic was associated with Protagoras without revealing a treatise’s structure or conclusion. This profile therefore uses Plato and Diogenes as named witnesses rather than windows onto an intact authorial system and checks their framing against specialist scholarship.',
  ], ['ancient-greek', 'epistemology'], ['plato', 'aristotle'], ['Truth or Overthrowing Arguments', 'On the Gods']),
  section('life-teaching', 'Abdera, itinerant teaching, and cautious chronology', [
    'Protagoras came from Abdera on the north Aegean coast and became associated with paid higher education across the Greek world, especially Athens. Plato’s Protagoras depicts him as an older, celebrated visitor surrounded by prospective students, and Plato’s Meno credits him with a long and lucrative career. These are strong evidence for reputation and professional identity, not account books. The familiar lifespan of about 490 to about 420 BCE is a reconstruction from such reports, including a claimed age and length of career. It should remain approximate rather than becoming false documentary precision.',
    'Later tradition credits Protagoras with drafting laws for the Athenian foundation at Thurii and tells competing stories about his teachers, fees, prosecution, exile, and death at sea. Some may preserve historical memory, but their combinations are not secure. The claim that Democritus taught him is chronologically doubtful, and named fees can function as moralizing signs of Sophistic wealth. What can be said more firmly is that Protagoras openly presented himself, in Plato’s staging, as a sophist who taught deliberative competence for household and civic affairs. Even that priority claim is preserved by a critic and should be reported as Protagoras’s represented self-description, not certified “firstness.”',
  ], ['ancient-greek', 'political-philosophy'], ['democritus', 'plato'], ['Protagoras', 'Meno']),
  section('measure-wording', 'What the measure sentence does—and does not—secure', [
    'The best-attested Protagorean wording makes a human being the measure of things that are and things that are not. Plato quotes it in Theaetetus 151e, Sextus also transmits it, and Diogenes repeats it. The noun anthrōpos can be rendered “human being” or collectively “humanity”; “measure” may concern a criterion, a determining perspective, or something more practical. The bare sentence does not specify whether the relevant measurer is each individual, a community, or the human condition generally. Translation cannot decide those philosophical questions in advance.',
    'Plato’s Socrates initially explains the formula through divergent perception: the same wind may feel cold to one person and not cold to another. He then connects Protagoras with a flux account and expands the thesis toward the truth of appearances and judgments. That development is indispensable evidence for ancient interpretation, but it is still Plato’s argument. No surviving Protagorean paragraph confirms every link among measure, perception, universal flux, and the impossibility of false belief. The safest formulation is therefore layered: the measure sentence is secure; an individual-relative reading was ancient and influential; the full theory attacked in the Theaetetus is a Platonic reconstruction whose historical reach remains disputed.',
    'The identity of the measurer changes the doctrine’s consequences. If each perceiver measures how things are for that perceiver, disagreement need not mean that one person misreports an experience. If a city measures justice through its current norms, individual dissent occupies a different relation to truth and authority. If “human” marks the limits of the species, the sentence may contrast human access with divine or mind-independent measure. The surviving line does not choose among these options, and Plato’s dialogue explores more than one. Interpretation should therefore state its proposed subject and domain instead of treating the English slogan as self-explanatory. The grammatical ambiguity is evidence to preserve, not an inconvenience a translator may silently remove.',
  ], ['epistemology'], ['plato', 'sextus-empiricus'], ['Truth or Overthrowing Arguments', 'Theaetetus']),
  section('better-worse', 'Truth, benefit, and the possibility of expertise', [
    'A crude reading says that if every appearance is true for its subject, every opinion must be equally good and teaching becomes pointless. The Theaetetus anticipates this objection. In the speech Socrates supplies on Protagoras’s behalf, a sophist resembles a doctor: one does not replace a false appearance with a true one so much as transform a worse condition into a better one. Cities likewise can be persuaded toward judgments that are more advantageous. This preserves a role for expertise, but it raises a difficult question about the standard by which better and worse are distinguished.',
    'The dialogue’s evidence does not settle one consistent Protagorean answer. Sensory appearances are discussed relative to individuals; judgments about justice are represented as holding for a city while it maintains them; predictions about future advantage appear open to expert correction. These may be parts of a nuanced view, tensions in Protagoras, or stages in Plato’s charitable reconstruction. Mi-Kyoung Lee treats the challenge to expert knowledge as central to Protagoras’s reception. For beginners, the important point is not that “anything goes,” but that situated judgment and improvement must be explained together. Relativity without improvement empties education; improvement without a standard beyond present appearance strains radical relativism.',
  ], ['epistemology', 'political-philosophy'], ['plato'], ['Theaetetus']),
  section('self-refutation', 'The self-refutation problem', [
    'Plato’s most famous objection asks what happens when most people judge that the measure doctrine is false. If Protagoras grants truth to each person’s judgment, must he also grant the truth of his opponents’ rejection? Theaetetus 170e–171c develops versions of this reversal, and Aristotle and Sextus preserve related anti-Protagorean arguments. The objection is powerful against an unrestricted thesis that every belief is true, but its force depends on how truth is relativized. A defender may distinguish “true for the opponent” from true without qualification, while a critic may argue that such added qualification no longer captures the doctrine’s own assertion.',
    'This is a reception history, not a transcript of Protagoras answering his successors. The lost author may have restricted measure to perception, accepted some objective claims about advantage, or lacked the later technical vocabulary of relativism. Conversely, ancient critics may preserve a real implication he embraced. The evidence does not authorize a clean victory speech. It authorizes a philosophical problem: can a doctrine respect disagreement without turning its own denial into an equal truth, and can it identify experts without quietly reintroducing objective standards? Protagoras matters because Plato, Democritus, Aristotle, and later epistemology had to sharpen their accounts of error and knowledge in response.',
  ], ['epistemology', 'logic'], ['plato', 'democritus', 'aristotle', 'sextus-empiricus'], ['Theaetetus']),
  section('argument', 'Opposed logoi and the “weaker” case', [
    'Diogenes reports that Protagoras held there are two opposed logoi on every matter. Aristotle says he was criticized for making the weaker logos stronger. Neither compact report licenses the modern slogan that Protagoras taught lying for hire. Logos can mean statement, account, case, or argument, and the comparative terms can concern argumentative standing rather than moral worth. The evidence does support training in adversarial presentation, refutation, and competing cases. It does not preserve a Protagorean handbook showing exactly how those exercises related to the measure doctrine.',
    'In assemblies and courts, an initially unpopular case may deserve its strongest presentation; the same technique can also disguise injustice. That ambiguity is educationally central. Protagorean antilogy can train a speaker to identify suppressed premises, anticipate objections, and recognize how a dispute looks from another position. It can also reward victory over inquiry. Plato and Aristotle emphasize the danger, while democratic institutions reveal why the skill was demanded. Calling Protagoras the inventor of debate would overstate late priority reports. Calling him a major early professional of opposed argument is more defensible and preserves the ethical question his reception poses: what governs persuasive competence when arguments can be built on both sides?',
  ], ['logic', 'political-philosophy', 'philosophy-of-language'], ['aristotle', 'plato'], ['Art of Controversy']),
  section('civic-education', 'Civic virtue in Plato’s Great Speech', [
    'In Plato’s Protagoras 320c–328d, Protagoras answers the challenge that civic virtue cannot be taught. A myth has Prometheus provide technical resources while Zeus distributes justice and a sense of restraint broadly enough for communities to survive. The prose explanation then describes education through family, poetry, music, schooling, law, and punishment. Citizens participate in political judgment because civic capacities are not concentrated like specialized crafts. The speech offers a powerful account of social formation: political life requires shared dispositions, and communities continually teach them even without one exclusive expert.',
    'Many scholars judge this material plausibly Protagorean, but Plato composed its literary setting and wording. The speech also does not amount to a modern theory of equal citizenship. Its assumed participants are free male citizens, and its account of punishment and exclusion reflects a Greek polis. A measured interpretation can still identify the philosophical advance without anachronism. Civic norms are neither effortless instincts nor merely private possessions; they are human achievements sustained by practices. Protagoras’s advertised expertise may lie in improving deliberation within that common education, not monopolizing virtue. The unresolved question is why one should pay him if everyone already teaches civic conduct—a difficulty Plato deliberately leaves alive.',
  ], ['political-philosophy', 'ethics'], ['plato', 'socrates'], ['Protagoras']),
  section('language-poetry', 'Language, speech acts, and poetic criticism', [
    'Ancient reports associate Protagoras with correctness of language, kinds of utterance, grammatical gender, and criticism of poetry. Aristotle says he objected that the opening invocation of the Iliad grammatically commands the goddess rather than prays, and later testimony credits classifications such as assertion, question, answer, and command. Because the technical works are lost, it is unsafe to present a finished Protagorean grammar. The reports more modestly show that form, force, and appropriateness of speech had become explicit subjects of teaching.',
    'Plato’s dialogue also stages Protagoras interpreting Simonides and detecting an apparent contradiction. Poetry was a major vehicle of education, so close verbal attention had ethical and civic stakes. Yet Plato’s Socrates turns the episode into a criticism of arguing through an absent poet, and the audience’s response belongs to the drama. Protagoras can be described as an early contributor to reflection on utterance and literary criticism without being called the founder of linguistics. His lasting contribution is the problem he exposes: speech is not a neutral wrapper for judgment. Grammatical form, genre, audience, and the force of an utterance influence what an argument does.',
  ], ['philosophy-of-language', 'logic'], ['aristotle', 'plato'], ['Protagoras', 'Poetics']),
  section('gods', 'Agnostic wording and an unreliable persecution narrative', [
    'The opening attributed to On the Gods says that Protagoras cannot know whether gods exist, do not exist, or what form they have, citing the obscurity of the subject and the brevity of human life. This is a first-person limit claim, not an explicit assertion that no gods exist. Its exact surrounding argument is lost. It can be called agnostic in a modern explanatory sense, provided that the label does not erase the Greek civic setting or turn suspension about knowledge into a complete theory of religion and ritual.',
    'Diogenes and other late sources tell of prosecution, expulsion, public burning of books, flight, and drowning. Their details conflict, and Plato’s Meno instead presents Protagoras as having enjoyed a durable reputation throughout a long career. Taylor and Lee therefore caution against narrating the persecution complex as established biography. It is evidence for how later antiquity remembered intellectual danger, not secure evidence for a fifth-century trial. A responsible timeline records no exact prosecution year. Protagoras’s religious fragment remains historically bold, but bold wording alone does not prove that Athens burned his books, forced him out, or caused his death.',
  ], ['philosophy-of-religion', 'epistemology'], ['plato'], ['On the Gods', 'Meno']),
  section('reading', 'How to read a philosopher preserved by opponents', [
    'Begin with the fragment collection, separating direct wording from testimonia. Then read Plato’s Protagoras for education and civic virtue, marking where dramatic characterization and Socratic argument shape the encounter. Read Theaetetus 151e–171c only after that, distinguishing the quoted measure sentence, Socrates’s examples, the speech he constructs for Protagoras, and the subsequent refutations. Diogenes Laertius is useful for the ancient dossier of titles and stories precisely when his distance and contradictions remain visible. This sequence prevents the measure doctrine from swallowing the rest of Protagoras’s educational practice.',
    'Keep four questions open rather than forcing a system: who or what is the human measure; how can some appearances be better without being truer; what ethical limits govern opposed argument; and what does intellectual modesty about gods permit in civic life? Consult Taylor and Lee for the whole Sophistic setting and Mi-Kyoung Lee for the epistemological afterlife. The goal is not to rescue Protagoras from every ancient criticism or repeat Plato’s verdict. It is to reconstruct the strongest responsible set of problems from sparse evidence while keeping the authority of each witness plainly and carefully labeled.',
  ], ['ancient-greek', 'epistemology', 'political-philosophy'], ['plato', 'aristotle', 'sextus-empiricus'], ['Protagoras', 'Theaetetus', 'On the Gods']),
];

