const prodicusSections: ArticleSection[] = [
  section('recovery', 'A teacher visible through other authors', [
    'Prodicus of Ceos was a prominent late-fifth-century sophist whose own works are lost. His reputation survives in Plato’s comic but attentive scenes, Xenophon’s retelling of an ethical performance, scattered remarks in Aristotle and other authors, and much later reports about religion and natural inquiry. That archive is rich enough to identify recurring interests but too mediated for a seamless doctrine. The central editorial distinction is between evidence that Prodicus was known for something and evidence for exactly what he said. Plato strongly witnesses a reputation for verbal discrimination; he does not provide stenographic lecture notes.',
    'Modern recovery has improved because Robert Mayhew collects far more evidence than the older Diels–Kranz dossier and specialist studies clarify the separate witness problems. That work also exposes how thin the direct remains are. No complete Prodicean book or speech survives. Xenophon alone gives a long continuous passage attributed to him, and Xenophon immediately qualifies its verbal fidelity. This profile consequently avoids treating every clever distinction made by Plato’s character, every sentence in Xenophon’s Heracles episode, or every later atheism label as a secure fragment of Prodicus.',
  ], ['ancient-greek', 'philosophy-of-language'], ['plato', 'socrates'], ['Fragments and testimonia']),
  section('life-career', 'Ceos, diplomacy, fees, and uncertain dates', [
    'Prodicus came from the island polis of Ceos and is represented as visiting Athens on public business as well as teaching there. Sophists could combine embassy, display speech, private instruction, and elite networks because civic representation depended on verbal skill. Plato places Prodicus among the older celebrated teachers gathered at Callias’s house. The evidence supports activity in the later fifth century BCE and likely overlap with Socrates; it does not establish the conventional 460–399 BCE endpoints as documented birth and death years. “Flourished in the late fifth century” is the more honest date display.',
    'Ancient sources associate Prodicus with paid lectures and different levels of instruction. Plato jokes elsewhere that Socrates could afford a one-drachma lecture but not an expensive full course, while other reports connect Prodicus with public displays. Exact sums belong to literary and retrospective testimony, so they should illustrate reputation rather than serve as audited prices. Likewise, calling Socrates his pupil overstates comic references to instruction. A safer account says that Plato repeatedly makes Prodicean verbal expertise a recognizable point of comparison for Socratic questioning. Professional success is secure in outline; a formal school, fixed curriculum, and teacher-pupil genealogy are not.',
  ], ['political-philosophy', 'philosophy-of-language'], ['socrates', 'plato', 'protagoras'], ['Protagoras']),
  section('synonymy', 'Near-synonyms as practical distinctions', [
    'Plato’s Protagoras 337a–c gives Prodicus a conciliating speech built from paired terms: participants should debate without quarreling, agree without merely sharing an opinion, and show esteem rather than praise indiscriminately. The scene is funny because Prodicus’s habit is conspicuous, yet the comedy depends on an intelligible reputation. Other Platonic passages similarly associate him with correctness of names and distinctions among related words. The evidence supports a practice of semantic discrimination more confidently than a comprehensive theory of meaning.',
    'Such distinctions matter when nearby words carry different evaluations. Courage may differ from uncomprehending boldness; equal distribution may differ from impartial judgment; bodily gratification may differ from intellectual enjoyment. Sorting terms can expose a slide in an argument or show that speakers only appear to agree. But a distinction is not automatically a discovery about reality. A teacher can stipulate usage, mistake contextual variation for fixed essence, or use verbal finesse as prestige. Prodicus’s importance lies in turning those risks into a method question: when does increased lexical precision improve practical judgment, and what evidence decides that a proposed boundary is worth preserving?',
  ], ['philosophy-of-language', 'logic', 'ethics'], ['plato', 'socrates'], ['Protagoras']),
  section('method-limits', 'What Plato’s comedy can and cannot prove', [
    'Plato is both the main witness for Prodicean synonymy and an author with reasons to subordinate it to philosophical dialectic. Prodicus’s interventions often settle verbal etiquette while Socrates pursues a definition or inconsistency. This contrast can imply that choosing the “correct” word is superficial unless the speaker can also explain the thing under discussion. Yet Plato repeatedly returns to Prodicus, which suggests that the practice was intellectually recognizable rather than invented for one joke. The historical inference should stop there: recurring characterization supports reputation, not the exact ownership of every distinction assigned to the character.',
    'A useful modern analogy is conceptual engineering only if it remains explicitly analogical. Prodicus did not possess modern semantics, corpus linguistics, or a theory of necessary and sufficient conditions preserved in text. His practice seems closer to disciplined attention within rhetoric, ethics, poetry, and education. Its best lesson is procedural. When a dispute turns on a word, ask whether participants use it in the same way, which evaluation the word imports, and whether a proposed distinction changes the practical issue. Then return to evidence. Precision serves inquiry only when it clarifies reasons rather than replacing them.',
    'The surviving examples also vary in evidential weight. A distinction embedded in Plato’s characterization may reflect an actual Prodicean habit, a recognizable exaggeration, or a Platonic use of that habit for another argument. A later scholium may preserve technical vocabulary while also systematizing it retrospectively. Convergence across witnesses is stronger evidence for the broad activity than any isolated pair of words is for a doctrine. That asymmetry should shape the page: “Prodicus was known for distinctions” can be stated firmly; “Prodicus defined this term exactly as follows” generally needs a named passage and a qualification about its source. Confidence belongs to the recurring pattern, not automatically to every surviving example.',
  ], ['philosophy-of-language', 'epistemology'], ['plato', 'aristotle'], ['Fragments and testimonia']),
  section('heracles-source', 'Whose words are in the Choice of Heracles?', [
    'In Memorabilia 2.1.21–34, Xenophon’s Socrates tells Aristippus a story he attributes to Prodicus. A young Heracles considering his life meets two women who offer rival paths, conventionally called Virtue and Vice. The episode is the longest surviving continuous presentation connected to Prodicus, but its transmission has several layers: Xenophon writes Socrates retelling Prodicus for a Xenophontic ethical argument. At the end, Xenophon calls what preceded an outline and says Prodicus clothed the thoughts in finer language. That statement secures attribution while warning against verbatim quotation.',
    'Scholars dispute how close the passage stands to Prodicus. David Sansone argues for unusually close wording; Vivienne Gray argues that its language is characteristically Xenophontic even if the gist is Prodicean. The disagreement matters because synonym choices inside the story cannot simply be added to Prodicus’s linguistic doctrine. This profile adopts the conservative common ground: Prodicus was associated with an ethical performance about Heracles’s choice, and Xenophon preserves a substantial adaptation; exact wording, full original structure, and the relationship to any work titled Seasons or Hours remain uncertain. The story’s later title should not be mistaken for a securely transmitted Prodicean book title.',
  ], ['ethics', 'philosophy-of-language'], ['socrates'], ['Xenophon, Memorabilia 2.1.21–34']),
  section('heracles-argument', 'Pleasure, effort, and the temporal shape of a life', [
    'Xenophon’s version does more than contrast pleasure with painful duty. Both speakers appeal to satisfaction. Vice promises immediate access to food, drink, sex, rest, and enjoyment without demanding productive effort. Virtue argues that valued goods become available through appropriate practices: the gods favor piety, friends require service, cities reward useful action, bodily capability requires training, and durable reputation follows achievement. The choice is therefore between organizations of desire across time. It asks whether a life of consumption without cultivation can sustain the very capacity for enjoyment it promises.',
    'This ethical argument is not neutral demonstration. Personification, appearance, names, and the distribution of future consequences guide the audience. Nor is hardship intrinsically good: the passage reflects an elite male Greek horizon of civic honor, agriculture, military fitness, and household success. It says little about coerced labor or people excluded from those opportunities. Read critically, however, the story raises a durable question without romanticizing suffering. Which present efforts build agency and reciprocal relationships, and which merely impose pain? Prodicean verbal care is relevant because each speaker redescribes pleasure, work, freedom, and happiness to make one life appear choiceworthy.',
  ], ['ethics', 'virtue-ethics'], ['aristotle'], ['Xenophon, Memorabilia 2.1.21–34']),
  section('attribution', 'The absent ending and the danger of familiar retellings', [
    'Modern summaries often say that Heracles chooses Virtue at the crossroads. Xenophon’s preserved episode as presented in Memorabilia ends after the rival speeches and Xenophon’s framing exhortation; it does not narrate an extended decision scene in Prodicus’s own voice. Other ancient reception makes the expected choice explicit, and the whole design strongly points toward it, but an article should distinguish implication and later formulation from surviving narration. Similarly, “crossroads” is a traditional name for the motif; Xenophon describes Heracles reaching the transition from youth and deliberating about which path of life to take.',
    'The correction is not pedantry. Familiar titles and images can create a false sense that an intact fable descended unchanged from Prodicus. In fact, the episode became a flexible moral emblem in later philosophy, literature, and art. Each reception sharpens the binary and may add scenery, names, or outcomes. The historically responsible object is a layered tradition: a Prodicean performance attributed by Xenophon, a Xenophontic adaptation used in Socratic exhortation, and a long reception of “Hercules at the crossroads.” This layering makes the story more educational, because students can see how transmission turns an argument into an icon.',
  ], ['ethics', 'aesthetics'], ['socrates'], ['Xenophon, Memorabilia 2.1.21–34']),
  section('religion', 'Useful things, culture heroes, and disputed atheism', [
    'Later sources attribute to Prodicus an account in which early humans applied divine names to life-sustaining things such as sun, rivers, crops, bread, and wine, and perhaps later honored discoverers or culture heroes who supplied benefits. No intact Prodicean treatise preserves that sequence. Sextus, Philodemus, Cicero, and doxographers transmit it within later debates about theology and atheism. The safest core claim is that Prodicus was credited with explaining religious naming through perceived benefit and cultural memory. The scope and intended conclusion remain disputed.',
    'Some ancient and modern readers infer atheism: if Demeter is grain and Dionysus wine, traditional personal gods have been reduced to useful things and remembered benefactors. Others allow a less radical interpretation or stress how later polemics may have classified Prodicus. Kouloumentas reconstructs a broader narrative of civilization linking agriculture, religion, and culture heroes; that is a scholarly proposal, not recovered chapter order. Calling Prodicus simply an atheist hides both the fragmentary evidence and disagreement. A more accurate profile says that he became an important ancient case for naturalistic genealogy of religious representation, with radical and moderate readings still contested.',
    'The religious dossier also illustrates why “naturalistic” and “atheistic” are not interchangeable. Explaining how a divine name arose can compete with traditional myth without answering whether any divine reality exists. Conversely, identifying a god with a useful material good may be more reductive than a merely historical account of naming. Because different reporters compress the theory for different polemics, the exact target is uncertain. The question should remain open at the point the sources leave it open: did Prodicus replace popular gods, reinterpret them, or supply opponents with premises from which they inferred denial?',
  ], ['philosophy-of-religion', 'ancient-greek'], ['sextus-empiricus'], ['Fragments and testimonia']),
  section('relations-reception', 'Prodicus among Sophists and Socratic authors', [
    'Prodicus shared a professional world with Protagoras, Hippias, Gorgias, and other itinerant experts, but “the Sophists” were not one school with a common doctrine. His distinctive surviving reputation concerns verbal discriminations, ethical performance, and a controversial genealogy of divine naming. Plato’s Socrates sometimes claims limited instruction from him while also turning him into a comic foil. That evidence supports contact, familiarity, and methodological comparison more securely than a claim that Prodicus formally trained Socrates or invented Socratic definition.',
    'Later philosophy repeatedly confronts the general problem he represents: moral and political disagreements can hide inside apparently shared vocabulary. That thematic legacy does not establish a direct line to Aristotle, modern analytic philosophy, or ordinary-language philosophy. Most of Prodicus’s works vanished, and later thinkers rarely inherit a documented Prodicean system. His importance is better stated without priority inflation. He is one early named practitioner who made fine verbal distinctions conspicuous within higher education and connected, at least in reception, the naming of goods with choices about how to live.',
  ], ['ancient-greek', 'philosophy-of-language', 'ethics'], ['protagoras', 'hippias-of-elis', 'gorgias', 'socrates', 'plato'], ['Protagoras', 'Memorabilia']),
  section('reading', 'How to read Prodicus without inventing a corpus', [
    'Start with Xenophon’s Memorabilia 2.1.21–34, marking the narrator, the attribution to Prodicus, the two persuasive speeches, and Xenophon’s closing qualification. Then read Plato’s Protagoras 337a–341e for synonymy and comic dramatic placement. Neither should be flattened into direct authorial prose. Use Mayhew or Laks and Most to see how short reports are classified and to compare sources on fees, language, natural inquiry, and religion. Gray’s article supplies a focused warning about inferring Prodicean wording from Xenophon.',
    'Carry four questions through the evidence. Which distinctions genuinely change an argument? What account of pleasure and effort does the Heracles adaptation defend? How much belongs to Prodicus rather than Xenophon’s Socratic project? What follows—and what does not—from explaining divine names through benefit? Kouloumentas offers a strong civilization reconstruction, while Taylor and Lee map the broader Sophistic setting. The result should remain historically proportionate: Prodicus was neither a trivial thesaurus nor a fully documented founder of secular linguistics. He is a difficult case in how language, ethical framing, and cultural explanation survive through philosophically interested witnesses.',
  ], ['philosophy-of-language', 'ethics', 'philosophy-of-religion'], ['socrates', 'plato'], ['Xenophon, Memorabilia 2.1.21–34', 'Protagoras', 'Fragments and testimonia']),
];

