const gorgiasSections: ArticleSection[] = [
  section('recovery', 'Two speeches, fragments, and a treatise known indirectly', [
    'Gorgias of Leontini is unusual among the older sophists because two substantial display speeches survive under his name: the Encomium of Helen and Defense of Palamedes. A fragment of a funeral speech and shorter sayings also remain. The philosophical work usually called On Not-Being or On Nature does not survive directly. Its arguments reach us through two different summaries, one in Sextus Empiricus and one in an anonymous work conventionally called On Melissus, Xenophanes, and Gorgias, or MXG. Those summaries overlap but diverge in wording, order, and arguments.',
    'This transmission requires separate levels of confidence. The Helen and Palamedes have manuscript problems, yet they provide continuous Gorgianic texts. On Not-Being must be reconstructed through epitomators whose philosophical vocabularies and purposes may reshape it. Plato’s Gorgias adds a fourth layer: a dialogue written by a critic, with a dramatic Gorgias who makes claims about rhetoric. No responsible profile can fuse those sources into one voice. The surviving speeches show what Gorgias’s prose can do; Sextus and MXG report a lost argument; Plato witnesses reputation and builds a moral critique.',
  ], ['ancient-greek', 'philosophy-of-language', 'epistemology'], ['plato', 'sextus-empiricus'], ['Encomium of Helen', 'Defense of Palamedes', 'On Not-Being or On Nature']),
  section('life-career', 'Leontini, embassy, performance, and approximate longevity', [
    'Gorgias came from Leontini in Sicily and is reported to have led an embassy to Athens in 427 BCE seeking help against Syracuse. His public speaking, teaching, festival performances, and travel made him a celebrated figure in mainland Greece. Sicily already had strong traditions of public argument and rhetorical reflection, but priority claims about inventing rhetoric are difficult to verify. Plato and later authors represent Gorgias as able to answer questions and train persuasive speech. That is good evidence for reputation; it does not yield a surviving curriculum or contract.',
    'Ancient biographers give Gorgias an exceptionally long life, producing conventional dates around 485 to around 380 BCE or variants such as 483–375. The endpoints depend on late reports and should be displayed as approximate. Claims that Empedocles taught him or Isocrates studied directly under him are also transmitted traditions rather than secure institutional records. His students and influence are better described through associations and reception. The embassy date anchors his public career more firmly than birth, death, or age at death. A cautious biography emphasizes attested activity across the late fifth and early fourth centuries without converting longevity stories into exact chronology.',
    'Fees and displays attract the same caution. Later accounts use wealth, statues, improvised speeches, and festival performances to characterize Gorgias’s fame. They plausibly reflect a public professional who cultivated memorable verbal presence, but exact earnings and “first inventor” anecdotes are conventional ways of ranking sophists. Plato’s Meno and Gorgias add conflicting pressures about what he promised to teach. The stable center is narrower: he taught and demonstrated persuasive speech, attracted students or associates, and became an emblem in disputes about rhetoric’s civic power. The moral scope of that instruction cannot be read directly from celebrity anecdotes.',
  ], ['ancient-greek', 'political-philosophy'], ['empedocles', 'plato'], ['Fragments and testimonia']),
  section('helen-form', 'The Encomium of Helen as argument and display', [
    'The Helen announces a task of removing blame from a figure conventionally condemned for the Trojan War. Gorgias organizes possible causes: divine necessity or fortune, physical force, persuasive speech, and love mediated by sight. If a stronger cause compelled Helen, the familiar moral verdict must be reconsidered. The speech is openly a display and ends by calling itself an amusement, but performance and philosophical seriousness are not exclusive. Its tightly arranged alternatives model how a speaker can reverse an inherited judgment by changing which causes the audience notices.',
    'The argument does not simply prove that Helen was innocent. Its list may omit relevant mixtures of agency, and its analogies can overstate compulsion. That possible excess is part of the exercise. Gorgias makes an audience experience the instability of confident blame while displaying the very verbal force under examination. The work should therefore be read at three levels: a paradoxical encomium or defense, an exploration of causal responsibility, and a demonstration of patterned prose. None alone exhausts it. Later labels such as “relativism” or “postmodernism” should not replace analysis of its numbered arguments and rhetorical form.',
  ], ['ethics', 'philosophy-of-language', 'aesthetics'], [], ['Encomium of Helen']),
  section('logos', 'What speech can do to belief and feeling', [
    'In Helen 8–14, logos is presented as tiny or imperceptible in body yet immense in effects. Poetry, incantation, argument, and persuasion can produce fear, pity, joy, confidence, and changed belief. Gorgias compares speech’s differentiated effects on the soul with drugs’ differentiated effects on the body. The comparison does not establish a modern psychological mechanism, but it makes form and response philosophically relevant. A hearer encounters not a bare proposition but rhythm, expectation, imagery, authority, and emotion organized in time.',
    'The analogy also complicates responsibility. If deceptive persuasion constrains a hearer, blame may shift toward the deceiver; yet Gorgias is himself deliberately persuading the audience. The speech does not provide a stable ethics of rhetoric that solves this reflexive difficulty. It demonstrates why one is needed. Persuasion can educate, console, terrify, or deceive, and its success is not identical with truth. That is stronger than the simplistic claim that Gorgias thought truth irrelevant. The text recognizes false speech and blameworthy deception while showing that human judgment remains vulnerable to speech’s causal power.',
  ], ['philosophy-of-language', 'philosophy-of-mind', 'ethics'], ['plato'], ['Encomium of Helen']),
  section('palamedes', 'Probability and the Defense of Palamedes', [
    'The Defense gives the mythical Palamedes a forensic response to an accusation of treason. He argues through feasibility, means, communication, motive, likely consequences, character, and the accuser’s lack of knowledge. Because direct witnesses are absent, the speech maps what would have needed to occur and presses contradictions in the alleged scenario. It is a sophisticated exercise in practical reasoning under uncertainty. The audience does not receive demonstrative access to the past; it must compare a charge with a structured account of possibility and probability.',
    'Palamedes also warns that a persuasive defense is not the same as knowledge. He must use words before judges who were not present, and reputation can be destroyed by an accusation. The work therefore complements the Helen. Speech is powerful, but it is also powerless to reproduce an event directly or guarantee recognition of truth. Arguments from character and likelihood can defend innocence or manufacture doubt. Calling the speech the invention of forensic logic would exaggerate priority. Calling it a major early surviving model of organized defense and evidential limitation is proportionate to what the text itself displays.',
  ], ['logic', 'political-philosophy', 'epistemology'], [], ['Defense of Palamedes']),
  section('style', 'Patterned prose is part of the reasoning', [
    'Gorgias became famous for balanced clauses, antithesis, parallel sound, rhythm, repetition, and verbal correspondence. Aristotle later criticizes some Gorgianic diction as excessively poetic, while Gorgias’s surviving prose shows why audiences noticed it. Style is not merely decoration attached after an argument. Antithesis makes alternatives feel exhaustive, parallelism turns different cases into comparable units, and rhythm helps an audience anticipate or remember a sequence. Form can reveal structure and can also make a weak division feel inevitable.',
    'That double capacity explains Gorgias’s importance for rhetoric and philosophy. Public reasoning always arrives in a perceptible form, even when a speaker claims to offer only facts. Attention, memory, expectation, and emotional response affect what a hearer can judge. Plato will demand that rhetorical power answer to justice and knowledge; Aristotle will analyze rhetorical proof and style more systematically. Gorgias should not be reduced to their precursor, but his prose makes their problems urgent. A reader can test each patterned sequence by rewriting it plainly: what clarity disappears, and what unsupported force becomes visible?',
  ], ['philosophy-of-language', 'aesthetics', 'logic'], ['plato', 'aristotle'], ['Encomium of Helen', 'Defense of Palamedes']),
  section('not-being-transmission', 'On Not-Being through Sextus and anonymous MXG', [
    'The lost treatise is commonly summarized in three movements: nothing is; even if something is, it cannot be known; even if it is known, it cannot be communicated to another. That formula is useful orientation, not a substitute for the transmitted arguments. Sextus’s version appears in Against the Logicians 1.65–87; the anonymous MXG account occupies 979a11–980b21 in Bekker numbering. Rodriguez emphasizes that the summaries diverge and that MXG contains serious lacunae, even though its language may stand closer to an earlier source in some respects.',
    'The transmission problem blocks confident claims about exact titles and sequence. On What Is Not and On Nature may be alternative formulations, and later numbering such as DK 82 B3 names a modern fragment dossier, not an ancient section authored by Gorgias. Critical collections print and translate the relevant evidence together so readers can compare. The profile therefore does not quote the “three theses” as pristine Gorgianic sentences. It reports them as the broad architecture preserved by two epitomes and attaches narrower claims to the specific Sextus or MXG locator where possible.',
    'Sextus writes within a later skeptical project and supplies explanatory connective tissue in his own terminology. The anonymous MXG author transmits a text closer in some linguistic respects to classical argument but through a damaged manuscript with lacunae and difficult syntax. Neither can simply be declared the original. Agreement between them strengthens a broad attribution; difference may reflect epitomizing, textual damage, or distinct access to the lost work. Editorial honesty therefore requires citing the transmitting author as well as Gorgias whenever an argument depends on one version’s special wording or order.',
  ], ['metaphysics', 'epistemology', 'philosophy-of-language'], ['parmenides', 'sextus-empiricus'], ['On Not-Being or On Nature']),
  section('not-being-interpretation', 'Nihilism, parody, dialectic, or several things at once?', [
    'If the three conclusions are read as Gorgias’s settled creed, the treatise seems self-undermining: a written argument communicates that nothing can be communicated. Older interpretations called this nihilism or despair; others treated the work as parody of Eleatic reasoning or an advertisement of argumentative power. Caston argues for taking its dialectical work seriously without assuming dogmatic endorsement. Gorgias can use an opponent’s assumptions to generate trouble about thought, falsehood, and objects. Irony and philosophical pressure need not cancel one another.',
    'The arguments also resist one uniform verdict because their parts target different connections. Imagining impossible scenes challenges the inference from being thought to being real. Differences between objects, perceptions, and words challenge simple transfer from world to mind to hearer. Some inferences are weak or depend on equivocation; others isolate durable problems. It is safest to say that On Not-Being critically tests Eleatic and representational assumptions, not that Gorgias conclusively proves nonexistence or denies every truth. “Nihilist,” “skeptic,” and “relativist” may name interpretations, but none should appear as an unqualified school membership.',
  ], ['metaphysics', 'epistemology', 'philosophy-of-language'], ['parmenides', 'protagoras'], ['On Not-Being or On Nature']),
  section('plato', 'Plato’s Gorgias: indispensable critic, separate voice', [
    'In Plato’s dialogue, Gorgias identifies rhetoric with producing persuasion in civic settings and distinguishes legitimate use from a student’s abuse, much as combat training can be misused. Socrates presses whether rhetoric produces conviction without knowledge and whether its teacher must teach justice. The character makes concessions that drive Plato’s larger exchange with Polus and Callicles. These passages are essential evidence for how Plato framed rhetorical education, but they are not authorial commentary by the writer of Helen and Palamedes.',
    'A second Platonic passage complicates claims about teaching virtue: Meno 95c represents Meno as saying Gorgias mocked those who promised to teach virtue and claimed instead to make capable speakers. Yet the dialogue Gorgias pushes him toward responsibility for students’ justice. The tension may reflect different dramatic purposes, a distinction between technical instruction and moral formation, or historical ambiguity. The safe conclusion is limited. Gorgias was strongly associated with teaching persuasive speech for public life; claims that he taught virtue, rejected ethics, or regarded technique as entirely value-neutral depend heavily on Plato’s philosophical staging.',
    'The dialogue’s later speakers should be kept separate as well. Polus and Callicles radicalize questions about power, pleasure, convention, and nature after Gorgias recedes from the conversation. Their positions cannot be listed as Gorgias’s doctrines merely because the work bears his name. Plato uses the encounter to test a whole civic culture of persuasion, not to provide one man’s intellectual biography. Reading only the dialogue can therefore make the historical Gorgias disappear behind Platonic arguments; reading only the speeches can miss how quickly their persuasive power generated questions about justice and education.',
  ], ['political-philosophy', 'ethics', 'philosophy-of-language'], ['plato', 'socrates'], ['Gorgias', 'Meno']),
  section('reading', 'How to read Gorgias across genres and witnesses', [
    'Begin with Helen and Palamedes in a modern critical translation, following their numbered sections and reading aloud enough to hear patterned prose. For each, separate the mythical speaker’s case, the work’s argumentative structure, and Gorgias’s display of verbal effect. Then compare the Sextus and MXG summaries of On Not-Being through Laks and Most. Use Rodriguez for the textual and structural differences and Caston for a dialectical interpretation. Do not treat the familiar three-part slogan as if it were the surviving Greek treatise.',
    'Read Plato’s Gorgias last, first focusing on 447a–461b where the character Gorgias speaks, then following how the dialogue expands beyond him. Ask which criticisms address claims found in the surviving speeches and which belong to Plato’s larger confrontation with rhetoric and power. Keep four tensions active: persuasion and truth, compulsion and responsibility, probability and knowledge, performance and philosophy. Gorgias remains important not because he founded every later rhetorical technique, but because his texts make the medium of public reasoning visible—its capacity to organize evidence, alter feeling, expose assumptions, and deceive.',
  ], ['ancient-greek', 'philosophy-of-language', 'epistemology'], ['plato', 'aristotle', 'sextus-empiricus'], ['Encomium of Helen', 'Defense of Palamedes', 'On Not-Being or On Nature', 'Gorgias']),
];

